Membership operators (in, not in) work only on iterable objects (string, list, tuple, set, dict, range) that contain multiple values, and always return True or False
# ✅ Membership operator chalta hai

"Sarim"          # String   (multiple chars!)
[1, 2, 3]        # List     (multiple values!)
(1, 2, 3)        # Tuple    (multiple values!)
{1, 2, 3}        # Set      (multiple values!)
{"name": "x"}    # Dict     (multiple keys!)
range(10)        # Range    (multiple numbers!)

Membership Operator (in / not in) ka Logic:
Yeh operator andar ghum ke check karta hai ki koi value maujood hai ya nahi.
Iske liye object ke andar ke elements pe jaana zaroori hota hai.

Logical Reason:
List, String, Tuple, Set, Dict --> Kai elements hote hain   --> Ghúm sakte ho → in chalta hai 
int, float, complex, bool  --> Sirf ek single value hota hai --> Andar kuch nahi → in nahi chalta 

s = "sarim"
>>> "s"  in s
True
>>> "s" not in s
False
-------------------------------------------
>>> a="PYTHON"
>>> "HON" in a
True
>>> "HON" not in a
False
>>> "ABC" not in a
True
>>> "ABC" in a
False
>>>

-----------------------------------------------------

>>> a="PYTHON"
>>> "PYTN" in a     (Sequence + Continuous dono zaroori hain)
False
>>> "PYTN" not in a
True

>>> "PYT" in a
True
>>> "TYP" in a
False

-----------------------------------------------------
>>> a="PYTHON"
>>> "PYT" in a [1:4:1]
False
>>> "PYT" in a [0:4:1]
True
>>> "PTO" in a [0:6:2]
True
>>> "NOH" in a [0:6:-1]
False
>>> "NOH" in a [-1::-1]
True
>>> "NHY" in a [-1::-2]
True
>>> "OTP" in a [-2::-2]
True


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
>>> a=[10, "Rossum", 3.5, 3+5j]
>>> "Ross" in a
False
>>> "Rossum" in a
True
>>> "Ross" in a[1]
True

"Ross" in a        → False   (List pe chal raha hai → exact element nahi hai)
"Rossum" in a      → True    (List pe chal raha hai → exact element hai)
"Ross" in a[1]     → True    (String pe chal raha hai → "Rossum" ke andar "Ross" hai)


